{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792697218,"nonce":"qgQ+zQGNVRBhOQl5xt5K1++/LkA=","message":"Native tessellated path-brush clip support and focused verification\n\nScope: image/glass/blur brushes emitted by native fill_path and stroke_path into PathVertex geometry. Standalone glass/backdrop primitives and independently prepared text/glyph streams stay on parent 732c3370857424d2e3c7ccfcde44fe0095471eda74dae4e5b415daca036738fe.\n\nImplementation:\n- Fill/stroke brush emission uses the same prepared push-time physical contours, fill rule and active-batch cache as native SDF primitives. Rectangular scissors remain separate, and scratch ancestors are stripped. One contour header is shared across path vertices and primitive consumers in that batch.\n- PathVertex has a one-based clip_shape pointer at byte 108, replacing one padding lane. Stride remains 120 bytes; _padding now has two lanes. Both vertex layouts and flat shader interpolants carry it. This is a native Rust struct-field change, not a shared ClipPath/CSS or primitive/image-instance GPU layout change.\n- The former inline path shader body is in src/shaders/path.wgsl. It reuses the existing shared shape evaluator through the current build composer. Identical circle/ellipse/rounded-rect helpers come from the existing prelude. The local path DT variant is generated from the same body with the same auxiliary-reader substitution already used for images. The deferred SDF shader-fork redesign is untouched.\n- Path binding 7 selects the auxiliary buffer or RGBA32Float texture, including MSAA creation and rebinding after texture growth. Direct path APIs refresh their own aux rows instead of depending on a preceding draw's upload.\n- Batch merges rebase vertex headers and legacy polygon references, and background layer-command path cursors follow prefixed geometry. Foreground projection retains its aux pool. Shared clip frames translate once across primitive/path consumers in tight targets.\n- has_ordered_draws retains the existing ordered-image predicate and adds path vertices with shape headers. Renderer/application gates consume those paths through the existing ordered renderer, preserving path-only group ranges and avoiding a second application path overlay. The primitive-only canvas replay fallback for auxiliary-backed draws remains in place.\n\nControls and corrections:\n- Before wiring, both initial app tests fail: (10,10) and (13,13) paint outside the expected circle/transformed rings. /private/tmp/blinc-path-brush-negative.log.\n- The first compile caught a scripted translation call inserted into bounds collection instead of slice translation; corrected before pixel verification.\n- After wiring, the merged/public-overlay control failed at (62,19), actual [18,0,237,255] versus blue. Audit found a legacy empty-exclusion branch that skipped aux upload and legacy path-only group processing that ignored path ranges. Newly clipped paths now use ordered dispatch. The direct API poison-row test independently guards fresh uploads, and the merged group control passes with 1x and 4x MSAA.\n\nFinal scoped validation, CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=2, primitive/glyph capacities 1024:\n- cargo test -p blinc_app path_brush_clip --lib -- --include-ignored --test-threads=1: seven pass in both native storage and forced DT modes, six GPU cases plus one CPU scratch-hash case. Coverage includes image/glass/blur path fills/strokes; all owners and streams; scissors and opacity groups; push-time affine/DPI composition and fill rules; tight shared headers; merged path cursors; a header beyond two auxiliary-texture rows, forcing growth from its one-row initial allocation; public overlay and 1x/4x MSAA APIs; direct path APIs after poisoning aux rows; two canvases and changed/empty frames through all three app entries; and empty/non-finite clips.\n- cargo test -p blinc_app native_path_clip --lib -- --include-ignored --test-threads=1: six existing primitive-stream cases pass in both modes, covering the shared frame-translation helper.\n- cargo test -p blinc_gpu --test integration path_brush_clip: three pass, including unchanged stride/offset, shared headers, owner drains, scratch stripping, merge rebasing and path-command cursors.\n- Relevant CPU regressions pass: native_path_clip (4), foreground_layers_projection_moves_paths_and_merge_rebases_cursors (1), foreground_rgba_image_only_boundaries_and_merge_keep_local_cursors (1).\n- cargo test -p blinc_gpu --lib path_shader_parses: passes, parsing and validating both storage/DT path shaders with naga.\n- Touched-file rustfmt with skip_children and git diff --check pass. Temporary capability overrides are restored. Logs: /private/tmp/blinc-path-brush-{normal,dt}-*.log; driver /private/tmp/blinc_path_brush_modes.py.\n\nThe path vertex pipeline uses ordinary vertex attributes in both modes; its relevant fork is the auxiliary reader. No redundant vertex-storage-only mode or workspace-wide/DSL/docs/platform gates ran. No browser/mobile pixel runs or timing/memory benchmarks.\n\nMemory and limits: no stride increase and no new contour copy per vertex or draw. Native headers use the existing three rows plus packed points, cached once per owner. Existing clip selection, flattening/input limits, fade behavior, stacked-shape approximations, path batch brush-uniform conventions and placeholder image/backdrop binding are unchanged. Fixtures deliberately use the existing placeholder binding and do not claim asset resolution or mixed-brush batching support. Nothing pushed.","files":null}]}